<!--
  Every user-visible string in the app.

  What is NOT in here, deliberately:

   * MODEL AND TIER NAMES, and FaceFusion's own option vocabulary (`hyperswap`, `arcface`,
     `v79`, "pixel boost"). They are the words FaceFusion's own documentation uses, and a
     translated model name makes the app harder to follow, not easier — there is nothing to
     search for and nothing to compare against upstream.
   * FILENAMES. `ModelPaths` and `ModelDownload` are string-heavy for reasons that have
     nothing to do with the user, and a `hyperswap_v79.bin` that reached this file would
     produce a download that 404s in one locale and works in every other.
   * THE LOG BOX AND THE BUG REPORT. They exist to be read by whoever fixes the bug. A
     translated stack of tier-fallback messages in a report is worse for everyone, including
     the person who filed it.
   * THE LAUNCHER LABEL. It is a Gradle manifest placeholder derived from whether
     ContentGate.kt exists, which is what keeps `main` and `dev` installable side by side.
     It is a brand name.
   * THE HTTP API's responses. `ApiServer` answers a machine, and a script that matches on
     an error string must not have it change with the phone's locale. The API asks
     ContentGate for its message through an English-forced Context, so the sentences still
     live here once rather than twice.

  The web UI at assets/webui.html is a SECOND, independent table: it is read on a different
  machine by someone who may not be holding the phone, so it follows the browser's
  Accept-Language rather than this.
-->
